If we assume FRBs are distributed throughout their host galaxies and throughout space, then the lowest \\rm DM_{FRB} values set a bound on the electron column density associated with the halos of the Milky Way and the typical host galaxy. This measurement is the focus of the manuscript. Table 1 provides a summary of the notation used in this paper.

### Constraints from Pulsars

We consider

\\rm DM_{pulsar}=\\rm DM_{ISM}^{\\delta}\\ +\\rm DM_{MW,halo}^{\\delta}\\ \\,  with \\rm DM_{ISM}^{\\delta} the ISM contribution and \\rm DM_{MW,halo}^{\\delta} the halo contribution. We then define an ISM-corrected quantity \\rm\\Delta DM_{pulsar}, which subtracts the total ISM contribution along the pulsar sightline,

\\rm\\Delta DM_{pulsar}=\\rm DM_{pulsar}-\\rm DM_{ISM}\\ . 

Most pulsars have unknown distances yet are expected to lie predominantly in the Galactic disk, with a scale height of 100 pc (Faucher-Giguere and Kaspi, 2006). Therefore, \\rm DM_{ISM} is generally larger than \\rm DM_{pulsar} and the majority of \\rm\\Delta DM_{pulsar} values will be negative. Any positive values could be attributed to the halo, and therefore the maximum \\rm\\Delta DM_{pulsar} yields a lower limit:

\\rm DM_{MW,halo}>\\rm max\\left[\\rm\\Delta DM_{pulsar}\\right]\\ . 

Such an analysis must allow for uncertainties in the modeling of \\rm DM_{ISM}, but for high Galactic latitudes these uncertainties are expected to be less than 10 pc cm{}^{-3}.

### Constraints from FRBs

\\rm DM_{FRB} has contributions from the ISM, the MW halo, cosmic gas, and the FRB host galaxy,

\\rm DM_{FRB}=\\rm DM_{ISM}+\\rm DM_{MW,halo}+\\rm DM_{cosmic}+\\rm DM_{host}\\ . 

Similar to the pulsars, we define an ISM-corrected measure:

\\rm\\Delta DM_{FRB}=\\rm DM_{FRB}-\\rm DM_{ISM}\\ . 

From the full distribution of \\rm\\Delta DM_{FRB}, we will examine the lowest values on the expectation that these have lower \\rm DM_{cosmic} contributions. For reference, an FRB at z=0.03(e.g. Marcote et al., 2020) has an average \\rm\\langle DM_{cosmic}\\rangle\\,\\approx\\,25pc\\,cm^{-3}.

The lowest values of \\rm\\Delta DM_{FRB} should also reflect the lowest combinations of \\rm DM_{MW,halo} and \\rm DM_{host}. We expect significant variations in the latter both due to the distribution of host galaxy masses and also from variations in the FRB location within the galaxy. We express \\rm DM_{host}^{min} as the minimum of this distribution which may be 10 to several tens pc cm{}^{-3}.

Regarding variations in \\rm DM_{MW,halo}, galaxy formation models tend to predict a nearly spherical distribution of gas, especially beyond the inner halo (but see Yamasaki and Totani (2020) which includes a non-spherical component). Spherically symmetric models of our Galaxy yield less than 10 pc cm{}^{-3} variations in \\rm DM_{MW,halo} even though the Sun is located off-center (Prochaska and Zheng, 2019). In the following, we will assume a single \\rm DM_{MW,halo} unless otherwise discussed. One recovers

\\rm DM_{MW,halo}+\\rm DM_{host}^{min}=min\\left[\\rm\\Delta DM_{FRB}\\right]\\ \\,  and therefore

\\rm DM_{MW,halo}<\\rm min\\left[\\rm\\Delta DM_{FRB}\\right]\\ . 

## 3 Kernel Density Estimation

KDE is a non-parametric technique that estimates an unknown density by constructing a kernel at each data point and summing their contributions. Owing to their shapes, the distributions of \\rm\\Delta DM_{pulsar} and \\rm\\Delta DM_{FRB} are each suited to a different class of KDE. \\rm\\Delta DM_{pulsar} has smooth edges and can be adequately modelled with a Gaussian kernel and a fixed bandwidth. The sharp edge of \\rm\\Delta DM_{FRB}, however, necessitates a varying bandwidth and a kernel with a steep cut-off.

In SS 3.1 we outline standard KDE and in SS 3.2 we describe the modifications for asymmetric, bandwidth-varying KDE.

